# NETWORK TOPOLOGIES : 

## What is Network Topology
Network topology is the way devices like computers, switches, and routers are connected in a network.  
Different topologies were created over time to solve problems like cost, reliability, performance, and scalability.

Evolution:
Bus → Ring → Star → Mesh → Tree  
Each new topology fixed the drawbacks of the previous one.

---

## BUS TOPOLOGY

Definition:  
All devices are connected to a single backbone cable. Data travels through that cable to reach all devices.

Why it came:  
Early networks needed a cheap and simple method to connect computers. It used less cable and was easy to install.

Advantages:
- Low cost  
- Simple setup  
- Uses less cable  
- Suitable for small networks  

Drawbacks:
- If main cable fails, entire network stops  
- Hard to troubleshoot  
- Performance becomes slow with many devices  
- Not scalable  

Status:
Mostly outdated and rarely used today.

---

## RING TOPOLOGY

Definition:  
Each device connects to two other devices, forming a circular structure. Data travels in one direction around the ring.

Why it came:  
Created to reduce collisions and improve performance compared to bus topology.

Advantages:
- Organized data transmission  
- Fewer collisions  
- Predictable performance  

Drawbacks:
- Failure of one device can stop the network  
- Hard to add or remove devices  
- Difficult maintenance  

Status:
Outdated and rarely used today.

---

## STAR TOPOLOGY

Definition:  
All devices connect to a central hub or switch. All communication passes through the central device.

Why it came:  
To improve reliability and make troubleshooting easier than bus and ring topologies.

Advantages:
- Easy to install and manage  
- Easy troubleshooting  
- One cable failure does not affect others  
- Scalable  
- Good performance  

Drawbacks:
- If central switch fails, network stops  
- Requires more cable  
- Higher cost than bus topology  

Status:
Most commonly used topology today.

---

## MESH TOPOLOGY

Definition:  
Each device connects to multiple devices. In full mesh, every device connects to every other device.

Why it came:  
To provide maximum reliability and avoid single point of failure.

